// Part C, § 13 to § 18. `sys(a, b, ..)` sets a system of formulas, one
// below the other, behind one brace (`main.mbt`).

///|
fn sec_13() -> Array[&IntoContent] {
  [
    part("C. The Theory of the Gravitation-Field"),
    sec(
      "Equation of motion of a material point in a gravitation-field. Expression for the field-components of gravitation.",
      13,
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|A freely moving body not acted on by external forces moves,
        $|according to the special relativity theory, along a straight line
        $|and uniformly. This also holds for the generalised relativity theory
        $|for any part of the four-dimensional region, in which the
        $|co-ordinates \{m("K_0")} can be, and are, so chosen that
        $|\{m("g_(mu nu)")} have special constant values of the expression
        $|\{r("4")}.
        $|
        $|Let us discuss this motion from the stand-point of any arbitrary
        $|co-ordinate-system \{m("K_1")}; it moves with reference to
        $|\{m("K_1")} (as explained in \{s(2)}) in a gravitational field. The
        $|laws of motion with reference to \{m("K_1")}, follow easily from the
        $|following consideration. With reference to \{m("K_0")}, the law of
        $|motion is a four-dimensional straight line and thus a geodesic. As a
        $|geodetic-line is defined independently of the system of
        $|co-ordinates, it would also be the law of motion for the motion of
        $|the material-point with reference to \{m("K_1")}; If we put
      ),
    ),
    eq("Gamma_(mu nu)^tau = -chr(mu nu, tau)", "45"),
    Prose(
      "we get the motion of the point with reference to \{m("K_1")} given by",
    ),
    eq(
      "(d^2 x_tau)/(d s^2) = Gamma_(mu nu)^tau (d x_mu)/(d s) (d x_nu)/(d s)", "46",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|We now make the very simple assumption that this general covariant
        $|system of equations defines also the motion of the point in the
        $|gravitational field, when there exists no reference-system
        $|\{m("K_0")}, with reference to which the special relativity theory
        $|holds throughout a finite region. The assumption seems to us to be
        $|all the more legitimate, as \{r("46")} contains only the
        $|\{em("first")} differentials of \{m("g_(mu nu)")}, among which there
        $|is no relation in the special case when \{m("K_0")}
        $|exists.
        $|
        $|If \{m("Gamma_(mu nu)^tau")} vanish, the point moves uniformly and
        $|in a straight line; these magnitudes therefore determine the
        $|deviation from uniformity. They are the components of the
        $|gravitational field.
      ),
    ),
  ]
}

///|
fn sec_14() -> Array[&IntoContent] {
  [
    sec("The Field-equation of Gravitation in the absence of matter.", 14),
    Prose(
      (
        $|In the following, we differentiate "gravitation-field" from
        $|"matter", in the sense that everything besides the
        $|gravitation-field will be signified as matter; therefore the term
        $|includes not only "matter" in the usual sense, but also the
        $|electro-dynamic field. Our next problem is to seek the
        $|field-equations of gravitation in the absence of matter. For this we
        $|apply the same method as employed in the foregoing paragraph for the
        $|deduction of the equations of motion for material points. A special
        $|case in which the field-equations sought-for are evidently satisfied
        $|is that of the special relativity theory in which
        $|\{m("g_(mu nu)")} have certain constant values. This would be the
        $|case in a certain finite region with reference to a definite
        $|co-ordinate system \{m("K_0")}. With reference to this system, all
        $|the components \{m("B_(mu sigma tau)^(rho.alt)")} of the
        $|\{sc("Riemann")}'s Tensor [equation \{rb("43")}] vanish. These
        $|vanish then also in the region considered, with reference to every
        $|other co-ordinate system.
        $|
        $|The equations of the gravitation-field free from matter must thus be
        $|in every case satisfied when all
        $|\{m("B_(mu sigma tau)^(rho.alt)")} vanish.
        $|
        $|But this condition is clearly one which goes too far. For it is
        $|clear that the gravitation-field generated by a material point in
        $|its own neighbourhood can never be transformed away by any choice of
        $|axes, \{em("i.e.")}, it cannot be transformed to a case of constant
        $|\{m("g_(mu nu)")}.
        $|
        $|Therefore it is clear that, for a gravitational field free from
        $|matter, it is desirable that the symmetrical tensors
        $|\{m("B_(mu sigma tau)^(rho.alt)")} deduced from the tensors
        $|\{m("B_(mu nu)")} should vanish. We thus get 10 equations for 10
        $|quantities \{m("g_(mu nu)")}, which are fulfilled in the special
        $|case when \{m("B_(mu sigma tau)^(rho.alt)")} all vanish.
        $|
        $|Remembering \{r("44")} we see that in absence of matter the
        $|field-equations come out as follows; (when referred to the special
        $|co-ordinate-system chosen.)
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|sys((partial Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha)/(partial x_alpha)
        #|    + Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta = 0,
        #|  sqrt(-g) = 1)
      ),
      "47",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|It can also be shown that the choice of these equations is connected
        $|with a minimum of arbitrariness. For besides \{m("B_(mu nu)")},
        $|there is no tensor of the second rank, which can be built out of
        $|\{m("g_(mu nu)")} and their derivatives no higher than the second,
        $|and which is also linear in them.
        $|
        $|It will be shown that the equations arising in a purely mathematical
        $|way out of the conditions of the general relativity, together with
        $|equations \{r("46")}, give us the \{sc("Newton")}ian law of
        $|attraction as a first approximation, and lead in the second
        $|approximation to the explanation of the perihelion-motion of mercury
        $|discovered by \{sc("Leverrier")} (the residual effect which could
        $|not be accounted for by the consideration of all sorts of disturbing
        $|factors). My view is that these are convincing proofs of the
        $|physical correctness of the theory.
      ),
    ),
  ]
}

///|
fn sec_15() -> Array[&IntoContent] {
  [
    sec(
      "Hamiltonian Function for the Gravitation-field. Laws of Impulse and Energy.",
      15,
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|In order to show that the field equations correspond to the laws of
        $|impulse and energy, it is most convenient to write it in the
        $|following \{sc("Hamilton")}ian form: —
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|sys(delta {integral H d tau} = 0,
        #|  H = g^(mu nu) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta,
        #|  sqrt(-g) = 1)
      ),
      "47a",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|Here the variations vanish at the limits of the finite
        $|four-dimensional integration-space considered.
        $|
        $|It is first necessary to show that the form \{r("47a")} is
        $|equivalent to equations \{r("47")}. For this purpose, let us
        $|consider \{m("H")} as a function of \{m("g^(mu nu)")} and
      ),
    ),
    dm("g_sigma^(mu nu) (= (partial g^(mu nu))/(partial x_sigma))"),
    Prose("We have at first"),
    dm(
      (
        #|delta H &= Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta delta g^(mu nu)
        #|    + 2 g^(mu nu) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha delta Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta \
        #|  &= -Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta delta g^(mu nu)
        #|    + 2 Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha delta (g^(mu nu) Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta)
      ),
    ),
    Prose("But"),
    dm(
      (
        #|delta (g^(mu nu) Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta) = -1/2 delta [g^(mu nu) g^(beta gamma)
        #|  ((partial g_(nu lambda))/(partial x_alpha) + (partial g_(alpha lambda))/(partial x_nu)
        #|  - (partial g_(alpha nu))/(partial x_lambda))]
      ),
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The terms arising out of the two last terms within the round bracket
        $|are of different signs, and change into one another by the
        $|interchange of the indices \{m("mu")} and \{m("beta")}. They cancel
        $|each other in the expression for \{m("delta H")}, when they are
        $|multiplied by \{m("Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha")}, which is symmetrical
        $|with respect to \{m("mu")} and \{m("beta")} so that only the first
        $|member of the bracket remains for our consideration. Remembering
        $|\{r("31")}, we thus have: —
      ),
    ),
    dm(
      (
        #|delta H = -Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta delta g^(mu nu)
        #|  - Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha delta g_alpha^(mu beta)
      ),
    ),
    Prose("Therefore"),
    eq(
      (
        #|cases(gap: #0.9em,
        #|  display((partial H)/(partial g^(mu nu))) = &-Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta,
        #|  display((partial H)/(partial g_sigma^(mu nu))) = &Gamma_(mu nu)^sigma)
      ),
      "48",
    ),
    Prose(
      "If we now carry out the variations in \{r("47a")}, we obtain the system of equations",
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|partial/(partial x_alpha) ((partial H)/(partial g_sigma^(mu nu)))
        #|  - (partial H)/(partial g^(mu nu)) = 0
      ),
      "47b",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|which, owing to the relations \{r("48")}, coincide with \{r("47")},
        $|as was required to be proved.
        $|
        $|If \{r("47b")} is multiplied by \{m("g_sigma^(mu nu)")}, since
      ),
    ),
    dm(
      "(partial g_sigma^(mu nu))/(partial x_alpha) = (partial g_alpha^(mu nu))/(partial x_sigma)",
    ),
    Prose("and consequently"),
    dm(
      (
        #|g_sigma^(mu nu) partial/(partial x_alpha) ((partial H)/(partial g_alpha^(mu nu)))
        #|  = partial/(partial x_alpha) (g_sigma^(mu nu) (partial H)/(partial g_alpha^(mu nu)))
        #|  - (partial H)/(partial g_alpha^(mu nu)) (partial g_alpha^(mu nu))/(partial x_sigma)
      ),
    ),
    Prose("we obtain the equation"),
    dm(
      (
        #|partial/(partial x_alpha) (g_sigma^(mu nu) (partial H)/(partial g_alpha^(mu nu)))
        #|  - (partial H)/(partial x_sigma) = 0
      ),
    ),
    Prose("or"),
    eq(
      (
        #|sys((partial t_sigma^alpha)/(partial x_alpha) = 0,
        #|  -2 kappa.alt t_sigma^alpha = g_sigma^(mu nu) (partial H)/(partial g_alpha^(mu nu))
        #|    - delta_sigma^alpha H)
      ),
      "49",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|or, owing to the relations \{r("48")}, the equations \{r("47")} and
        $|\{r("34")},
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|kappa.alt t_sigma^alpha = 1/2 delta_sigma^alpha g^(mu nu) Gamma_(mu beta)^lambda Gamma_(nu lambda)^beta
        #|  - g^(mu nu) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu sigma)^beta
      ),
      "50",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|It is to be noticed that \{m("t_sigma^alpha")} is not a tensor, so
        $|that the equation \{r("49")} holds only for systems for which
        $|\{m("sqrt(-g) = 1")}. This equation expresses the laws of
        $|conservation of impulse and energy in a gravitation-field. In fact,
        $|the integration of this equation over a \{em("three-dimensional")}
        $|volume \{m("V")} leads to the four equations
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|d/(d x_4) {integral t_sigma^4 d V}
        #|  = integral (t_sigma^1 alpha_1 + t_sigma^2 alpha_2 + t_sigma^3 alpha_3) d S
      ),
      "49a",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|where \{m("a_1, a_2, a_3")} are the direction-cosines of the inward
        $|drawn normal to the surface-element \{m("d S")} in the Euclidean
        $|Sense. We recognise in this the usual expression for the laws of
        $|conservation. We denote the magnitudes \{m("t_sigma^alpha")} as the
        $|energy-components of the gravitation-field.
        $|
        $|I will now put the equation \{r("47")} in a third form which will be
        $|very serviceable for a quick realisation of our object. By
        $|multiplying the field-equations \{r("47")} with
        $|\{m("g^(nu sigma)")}, these are obtained in the "mixed" forms. If we
        $|remember that
      ),
    ),
    dm(
      (
        #|g^(nu sigma) (partial Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha)/(partial x_alpha)
        #|  = partial/(partial x_alpha) (g^(nu sigma) Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha)
        #|  - (partial g^(nu sigma))/(partial x_alpha) Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha
      ),
    ),
    Prose("which owing to \{r("34")} is equal to"),
    dm(
      (
        #|partial/(partial x_alpha) (g^(nu sigma) Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha)
        #|  - g^(nu beta) Gamma_(alpha beta)^sigma Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha
        #|  - g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(beta alpha)^nu Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha
      ),
    ),
    Prose("or slightly altering the notation equal to"),
    dm(
      (
        #|partial/(partial x_alpha) (g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha)
        #|  - g^(m n) Gamma_(m beta)^sigma Gamma_(n mu)^beta
        #|  - g^(nu sigma) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta
      ),
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The third member of this expression cancel with the second member of
        $|the field-equations \{r("47")}. In place of the second term of this
        $|expression, we can, on account of the relations \{r("50")}, put
      ),
    ),
    dm("kappa.alt (t_mu^sigma - 1/2 delta_mu^sigma t)"),
    Prose(
      (
        $|where \{m("(t = t_alpha^alpha)")}
        $|
        $|Therefore in the place of the equations \{r("47")}, we obtain
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|sys(partial/(partial x_alpha) (g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha)
        #|    = -kappa.alt (t_mu^sigma - 1/2 delta_mu^sigma t),
        #|  sqrt(-g) = 1)
      ),
      "51",
    ),
  ]
}

///|
fn sec_16() -> Array[&IntoContent] {
  [
    sec("General formulation of the field-equation of Gravitation.", 16),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The field-equations established in the preceding paragraph for
        $|spaces free from matter is to be compared with the equation
      ),
    ),
    dm("Delta phi = 0"),
    Prose(
      (
        $|of the \{sc("Newton")}ian theory. We have now to find the equations
        $|which will correspond to \{sc("Poisson")}'s Equation
      ),
    ),
    dm("Delta phi = 4 pi kappa.alt rho.alt"),
    Prose(
      (
        $|(\{m("rho.alt")} signifies the density of matter) .
        $|
        $|The special relativity theory has led to the conception that the
        $|inertial mass is no other than energy. It can also be fully
        $|expressed mathematically by a symmetrical tensor of the second rank,
        $|the energy-tensor. We have therefore to introduce in our generalised
        $|theory an energy-tensor \{m("T_sigma^alpha")} associated with
        $|matter, which like the energy components \{m("t_sigma^alpha")} of
        $|the gravitation-field (equations \{rb("49")}, and \{rb("50")}) have
        $|a mixed character but which however can be connected with
        $|symmetrical covariant tensors. The equation \{r("51")}
        $|teaches us how to introduce the energy-tensor (corresponding to the
        $|density \{m("rho.alt")} of \{sc("Poisson")}'s equation) in the field
        $|equations of gravitation. If we consider a complete system (for
        $|example the Solar-system) its total mass, as also its total
        $|gravitating action, will depend on the total energy of the system,
        $|ponderable as well as gravitational.
        $|
        $|This can be expressed, by putting in \{r("51")}, in place of
        $|energy-components \{m("t_mu^sigma")} of gravitation-field alone the
        $|sum of the energy-components of matter and gravitation,
        $|\{em("i.e.")}, \{m("t_mu^sigma + T_mu^sigma")}.
        $|
        $|We thus get instead of \{r("51")}, the tensor-equation
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|sys(partial/(partial x_alpha) (g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha)
        #|    = -kappa.alt ((t_mu^sigma + T_mu^sigma) - 1/2 delta_mu^sigma (t + T)),
        #|  sqrt(-g) = 1)
      ),
      "52",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|where \{m("T = T_mu^mu")} (\{sc("Laue")}'s Scalar). These are the
        $|general field-equations of gravitation in the mixed form. In place
        $|of \{r("47")}, we get by working backwards the system
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|sys((partial Gamma_(mu nu)^alpha)/(partial x_alpha)
        #|    + Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha Gamma_(nu alpha)^beta
        #|    = -kappa.alt (T_(mu nu) - 1/2 g_(mu nu) T),
        #|  sqrt(-g) = 1)
      ),
      "53",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|It must be admitted, that this introduction of the energy-tensor of
        $|matter cannot be justified by means of the Relativity-Postulate
        $|alone; for we have in the foregoing analysis deduced it from the
        $|condition that the energy of the gravitation-field should exert
        $|gravitating action in the same way as every other kind of energy.
        $|The strongest ground for the choice of the above equation however
        $|lies in this, that they lead, as their consequences, to equations
        $|expressing the conservation of the components of total energy (the
        $|impulses and the energy) which exactly correspond to the equations
        $|\{r("49")} and \{r("49a")}. This shall be shown afterwards.
      ),
    ),
  ]
}

///|
fn sec_17() -> Array[&IntoContent] {
  [
    sec("The laws of conservation in the general case.", 17),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The equations \{r("52")} can be easily so transformed that the
        $|second member on the right-hand side vanishes. We reduce
        $|\{r("52")} with reference to the indices \{m("mu")} and
        $|\{m("sigma")} and subtract the equation so obtained after
        $|multiplication with \{m("1/2 delta_mu^sigma")} from \{r("52")}. We
        $|obtain:
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|partial/(partial x_alpha) (g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha
        #|  - 1/2 delta_mu^sigma g^(lambda beta) Gamma_(lambda beta)^alpha)
        #|  = -kappa.alt (t_mu^sigma + T_mu^sigma)
      ),
      "52a",
    ),
    Prose("we operate on it by \{m("partial slash partial x_sigma")}. Now,"),
    dm(
      (
        #|(partial^2)/(partial x_alpha partial x_sigma) (g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha)
        #|  = -1/2 (partial^2)/(partial x_alpha partial x_sigma) [g^(sigma beta) g^(alpha lambda)
        #|  ((partial g_(mu lambda))/(partial x_beta) + (partial g_(beta lambda))/(partial x_mu)
        #|  - (partial g_(mu beta))/(partial x_lambda))]
      ),
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The first and the third member of the round bracket lead to
        $|expressions which cancel one another, as can be easily seen by
        $|interchanging the summation-indices \{m("alpha")} and
        $|\{m("sigma")} on the one hand, and \{m("beta")} and \{m("gamma")}
        $|on the other. The second term can be transformed according to
        $|\{r("31")}. So that we get
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      (
        #|(partial^2)/(partial x_alpha partial x_sigma) (g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha)
        #|  = 1/2 (partial^3 g^(alpha beta))/(partial x_alpha partial x_beta partial x_mu)
      ),
      "54",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The second member of the expression on the left-hand side of
        $|\{r("52a")} leads first to
      ),
    ),
    dm(
      "-1/2 (partial^2)/(partial x_alpha partial x_sigma) (g^(lambda beta) Gamma_(lambda beta)^alpha)",
    ),
    Prose("or"),
    dm(
      (
        #|1/4 (partial^2)/(partial x_alpha partial x_mu) [g^(lambda beta) g^(alpha delta)
        #|  ((partial g_(delta gamma))/(partial x_beta) + (partial g_(delta beta))/(partial x_lambda)
        #|  - (partial g_(lambda beta))/(partial x_delta))]
      ),
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The expression arising out of the last member within the round
        $|bracket vanishes according to \{r("29")} on account of the choice of
        $|axes. The two others can be taken together and give us on account of
        $|\{r("31")}, the expression
      ),
    ),
    dm(
      "-1/2 (partial^3 g^(alpha beta))/(partial x_alpha partial x_beta partial x_mu)",
    ),
    Prose("So that remembering \{r("54")} we have the identity"),
    eq(
      (
        #|(partial^2)/(partial x_alpha partial x_sigma) (g^(sigma beta) Gamma_(mu beta)^alpha
        #|  - 1/2 delta_mu^sigma g^(gamma beta) Gamma_(lambda beta)^alpha) equiv 0
      ),
      "55",
    ),
    Prose("From \{r("55")} and \{r("52a")} it follows that"),
    eq("(partial (t_mu^sigma + T_mu^sigma))/(partial x_sigma) = 0", "56"),
    Prose(
      (
        $|From the field equations of gravitation, it also follows that the
        $|conservation-laws of impulse and energy are satisfied. We see it
        $|most simply following the same reasoning which lead to equations
        $|\{r("49a")}; only instead of the energy-components
        $|\{m("t_mu^sigma")} of the gravitational-field, we are to introduce
        $|the total energy-components of matter and gravitational field.
      ),
    ),
  ]
}

///|
fn sec_18() -> Array[&IntoContent] {
  [
    sec(
      "The Impulse-energy law for matter as a consequence of the field-equations.",
      18,
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|If we multiply \{r("53")} with
        $|\{m("partial g^(mu nu) slash partial x_sigma")}, we get in a way
        $|similar to \{s(15)}, remembering that
      ),
    ),
    dm("g_(mu nu) (partial g^(mu nu))/(partial x_sigma)"),
    Prose("vanishes, the equations"),
    dm(
      (
        #|(partial t_sigma^alpha)/(partial x_alpha)
        #|  + 1/2 (partial g^(mu nu))/(partial x_sigma) T_(mu nu) = 0
      ),
    ),
    Prose("or remembering \{r("56")}"),
    eq(
      (
        #|(partial T_sigma^alpha)/(partial x_alpha)
        #|  + 1/2 (partial g^(mu nu))/(partial x_sigma) T_(mu nu) = 0
      ),
      "57",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|A comparison with \{r("41b")} shows that these equations for the
        $|above choice of co-ordinates asserts nothing but the vanishing of
        $|the divergence of the tensor of the energy-components of matter.
        $|
        $|Physically the appearance of the second term on the left-hand side
        $|shows that for matter alone the law of conservation of impulse and
        $|energy cannot hold; or can only hold when \{m("g^(mu nu)")} are
        $|constants; \{em("i.e.")}, when the field of gravitation vanishes.
        $|The second member is an expression for impulse and energy which the
        $|gravitation-field exerts per time and per volume upon matter. This
        $|comes out clearer when instead of \{r("57")} we write it in the Form
        $|of \{r("41")}.
      ),
    ),
    eq(
      "(partial T_sigma^alpha)/(partial x_alpha) = -Gamma_(sigma beta)^alpha T_alpha^beta",
      "57a",
    ),
    Prose(
      (
        $|The right-hand side expresses the interaction of the energy of the
        $|gravitational-field on matter. The field-equations of gravitation
        $|contain thus at the same time 4 conditions which are to be satisfied
        $|by all material phenomena. We get the equations of the material
        $|phenomena completely when the latter is characterised by four other
        $|differential equations independent of one another.
      ),
    ),
  ]
}
